finding: Declarations are not as narrow or host-correct as claimed. src/standard/physics/entity-reference.node.ts:86 declares /endpoint unavailable/, accepting an unrelated subsystem's warning; an independent temporary test using that declaration and console.warn("unrelated subsystem endpoint unavailable") passes. Other avoidably broad declarations include entity-reference.node.ts:33 (any Shape), standard/rendering/photometric-lights.node.ts:279 (any casting entity), and standard/rendering/cluster.node.ts:394 (any ignored count). src/core/mesh/register.node.ts:189-195 conditions the expected failed-build notice on the owner's adapter classification, but line 204 supplies only device, not adapter: requestGPU stamps that acquisition unidentified on every host (src/engine/runtime/gpu.ts:1286-1288), and createTestGpuApp cannot declare after a rejected build. Thus a real-adapter owner leaves the refused-build test with an undeclared warning; the passing local run does not cover that host because its owner is unidentified. Raw platform-dependent optional declarations at src/engine/app/system-errors.node.ts:167 and src/standard/rendering/overflow.node.ts:17 also permit either notice regardless of actual acquisition verdict, contrary to the requested host-specific declaration rule.
